.TH "FLOX\-PUBLISH" "1" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
flox\-publish \- Publish packages for Flox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
flox [<general\-options>] publish
     [\-d=<path>]
     [\-o=<org>]
     [\-\-signing\-private\-key <path>]
     [\-\-stability <stability>]
     [<package>]...
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Publish the specified \f[CR]<package>\f[R] from the environment in
\f[CR]<path>\f[R], uploading package metadata and copying the packages
so that it is available in the Flox Catalog.
.SS Preconditions
Flox makes some assertions before publishing, specifically:
.IP \(bu 2
The Flox environment used to build the package is tracked as a git
repository.
.IP \(bu 2
Tracked files in the repository are all clean.
.IP \(bu 2
The repository has a remote defined and the current revision has been
pushed to it.
.IP \(bu 2
The build environment must have at least one package installed.
.PP
These conditions ensure that the package being built can be located,
built, and reproduced in the future.
.SS Publishing process
Possible values for \f[CR]<package>\f[R] are all keys under the
\f[CR]build\f[R] attribute in \f[CR]manifest.toml\f[R].
If only one build is defined in \f[CR]manifest.toml\f[R], specifying the
\f[CR]<package>\f[R] is unnecessary.
If there are multiple builds defined, you may only publish a single
package at a time and must specify the name when calling
\f[CR]flox publish\f[R].
.PP
Flox will then perform a clone of the repository to a temporary location
and perform a clean \f[CR]flox build\f[R] operation.
This ensures that all files required to build the package are included
in the git repository.
.PP
When publishing a package, metadata is sent to Flox servers so that
information about the package can be made available in
\f[CR]flox install\f[R], \f[CR]flox search\f[R], and
\f[CR]flox show\f[R].
The package itself, along with any other packages it depends on, are
uploaded to the Catalog\(cqs configured Catalog Store.
By default, Flox provides and configures a Catalog Store, but you may
optionally provide your own Catalog Store.
Contact Flox directly if you\(cqre interested in this option.
.PP
Finally, the package is uploaded to the default Catalog, which is named
after your user, but you may specify the catalog to publish to via the
\f[CR]\-\-catalog\f[R] option.
.SS After publishing
After the package is published, it will be available to the
\f[CR]flox install\f[R], \f[CR]flox search\f[R], and
\f[CR]flox show\f[R] commands.
The package will appear with a name of the form
\f[CR]<catalog>/<name>\f[R] where \f[CR]<catalog>\f[R] is the name of
the catalog it was published to, and \f[CR]<name>\f[R] is the name of
the package as it was defined in the \f[CR][build]\f[R] section of the
manifest.
The \f[CR]<catalog>\f[R] name is either your user name or the name of
the organization that owns the Catalog.
.PP
For instance, if a user \f[CR]myuser\f[R] published a package called
\f[CR]hello\f[R] to their personal Catalog, the package would appear in
\f[CR]flox search\f[R] as \f[CR]myuser/hello\f[R].
.PP
When installing the package, it is downloaded directly from the Catalog
Store that it was published to.
.PP
Published packages on FloxHub are also available as catalog imports for
Nix expression builds.
Other environments can declare a FloxHub catalog in
\f[B]\f[CB]nix\-builds.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R] and reference the published
packages directly from \f[CR].nix\f[R] expressions under
\f[CR].flox/pkgs/\f[R].
.SS Sharing published packages
A package published to an individual user\(cqs Catalog may only be seen
and installed by that user.
In order to share packages with other users you must create an
organization.
See \f[B]the organizations concept
page\f[R] <\f[I]https://flox.dev/docs/concepts/organizations/\f[R]> for
more details on organizations and how to create them.
Note that this is a paid feature available with Flox for Teams.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[CR]<package>\f[R]
The package to publish.
Possible values are all keys under the \f[CR]build\f[R] attribute in the
environment\(cqs \f[CR]manifest.toml\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-o, \-\-org <org>\f[R]
Specify the organization to which an artifact should be published to.
Takes precedence over the default value of the user\(cqs GitHub handle.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-signing\-private\-key <path>\f[R]
The private key to use in signing the package during upload.
This is a local file path.
This option is only necessary when using a Catalog Store not provided by
Flox.
Takes precedence over the value of
\f[CR]publish.signing_private_key\f[R] from `flox config'.
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-stability <stability>\f[R]
Perform a nix expression build using a base package set of the given
stability as tracked by the catalog server.
A stability (e.g., \f[CR]\(dqstable\(dq\f[R]) identifies a curated
nixpkgs revision managed by the catalog server.
When omitted, the base package set is derived from the environment\(cqs
\f[CR]toplevel\f[R] group; if no \f[CR]toplevel\f[R] group exists, the
\f[CR]\(dqstable\(dq\f[R] stability is used by default.
An explicit \f[CR]\-\-stability\f[R] value overrides both of these
defaults.
Cannot be used with manifest builds.
.SS Environment Options
If no environment is specified for an environment command, the
environment in the current directory or the active environment that was
last activated is used.
.TP
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]
Path containing a .flox/ directory.
.SS General Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
Prints help information.
.PP
The following options can be passed when running any \f[CR]flox\f[R]
subcommand but must be specified \f[I]before\f[R] the subcommand.
.TP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase logging verbosity.
Invoke multiple times for increasing detail.
.TP
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Silence logs except for errors.
